---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PasswordStrengthVisualizer from '../../components/PasswordStrengthVisualizer';
---

<Layout title="Password Strength Visualizer" description="Test your password strength and see how long it takes a hacker to crack it.">
  <div class="bg-indigo-50 py-12 border-b border-indigo-100">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 text-center">
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-indigo-900 tracking-tight mb-4">
        Password Strength Visualizer
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-indigo-800">
        Type a password to see exactly how its mathematical complexity (entropy) affects how long a hacker would need to crack it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    <PasswordStrengthVisualizer client:load />

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto mt-12 bg-white border border-slate-200 p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Why length matters more than complexity</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">
        Many websites force you to create passwords like <code>P@ssw0rd1!</code> by requiring a mix of uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and symbols. While this adds complexity, the password is still short. A modern cracking rig can guess a short, complex password very quickly.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">
        However, if you use a "passphrase" made of multiple random words (like <code>correct horse battery staple</code>), the sheer length of the password makes the mathematical combinations so massive that it would take centuries to crack, even without special symbols.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
        <a href="/tools/password-generator" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center bg-indigo-600 hover:bg-indigo-700 text-white font-bold py-2 px-6 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          Use the Secure Password Generator
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
